  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_CH11_DIAGRAM_Q02 - EcoRI recognition site and sticky ends</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p>The diagram below illustrates the action of the restriction endonuclease <em>Eco</em>RI on a palindromic DNA sequence. Based on the cut sites indicated by the arrows, between which two bases does <em>Eco</em>RI cleave each strand, and what type of ends are generated?</p>
      <p>
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 170" width="460" height="170">
          <rect x="10" y="10" width="440" height="150" rx="8" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#cbd5e1" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="55" y="62" font-family="monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#1e293b">5' —</text>
          <text x="55" y="112" font-family="monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#1e293b">3' —</text>
          <rect x="105" y="42" width="240" height="30" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <rect x="105" y="92" width="240" height="30" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="125" y="63" font-family="monospace" font-size="17" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f172a">G   A   A   T   T   C</text>
          <text x="125" y="113" font-family="monospace" font-size="17" font-weight="bold" fill="#0f172a">C   T   T   A   A   G</text>
          <text x="355" y="62" font-family="monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#1e293b">— 3'</text>
          <text x="355" y="112" font-family="monospace" font-size="16" font-weight="bold" fill="#1e293b">— 5'</text>
          <line x1="145" y1="22" x2="145" y2="40" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <polygon points="145,42 140,34 150,34" fill="#dc2626"/>
          <line x1="305" y1="142" x2="305" y2="124" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <polygon points="305,122 300,130 310,130" fill="#dc2626"/>
          <text x="230" y="148" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" fill="#475569" text-anchor="middle">EcoRI Recognition Sequence</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Layout</strong><br/>The diagram shows the double-stranded palindromic sequence \(5'\text{--GAATTC--}3'\) paired with \(3'\text{--CTTAAG--}5'\). The top arrow points between G and A on the upper \(5' \to 3'\) strand, and the bottom arrow points between A and G (which is between G and A when read in the \(5' \to 3'\) direction) on the lower strand.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Governing Principle</strong><br/>Restriction endonucleases such as <em>Eco</em>RI inspect DNA until they locate their specific palindromic recognition site and cut both sugar-phosphate backbones a little away from the centre of the palindrome.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>Cleaving between G and A on both strands leaves single-stranded overhanging stretches of four bases (AATT) at the \(5'\) ends. Because these single-stranded overhangs can form hydrogen bonds with their complementary cut counterparts, they are called sticky ends.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Final Conclusion</strong><br/><em>Eco</em>RI cuts between G and A on both strands, generating \(5'\) overhanging sticky ends.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Between G and A on both strands, producing sticky ends.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Between A and T on both strands, producing blunt ends.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Between G and A on the upper strand and T and C on the lower strand, producing blunt ends.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Between T and C on both strands, producing sticky ends.</p>]]></text></answer>
