          <rect x="115" y="45" width="130" height="35" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2"/>
          <path d="M 115 80 Q 130 72 145 80 T 175 80 T 205 80 T 235 80 L 245 80" fill="none" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="2"/>
          <line x1="180" y1="22" x2="180" y2="185" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <rect x="150" y="165" width="60" height="8" fill="#475569"/>
          <rect x="145" y="195" width="70" height="6" fill="#64748b"/>
          <line x1="180" y1="201" x2="180" y2="232" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <circle cx="155" cy="145" r="3" fill="#0284c7"/>
          <circle cx="195" cy="150" r="3" fill="#0284c7"/>
          <circle cx="170" cy="120" r="3" fill="#0284c7"/>
          <circle cx="205" cy="115" r="3" fill="#0284c7"/>
          <circle cx="145" cy="100" r="3" fill="#0284c7"/>
          <circle cx="190" cy="95" r="3" fill="#0284c7"/>
          <text x="180" y="244" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#1e293b" text-anchor="middle">Sterile Air Sparger</text>
          <text x="260" y="75" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#334155">Gas entrainment</text>
          <text x="260" y="130" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#0284c7">Air bubbles</text>
        </svg>
      </p>
    ]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[
      <p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Layout</strong><br/>The schematic shows a sparged stirred-tank bioreactor where sterile air is introduced through a sparger at the bottom, rising as numerous fine bubbles through the liquid medium while an agitator rotates and gas entrainment occurs at the wavy liquid surface.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 2: Governing Principle</strong><br/>Aerobic microbial or eukaryotic cell cultures in large volumes (100–1000 litres) require continuous and efficient transfer of dissolved oxygen into the liquid medium.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 3: Step-by-Step Working</strong><br/>By sparging sterile air as small bubbles through the liquid, the total gas-liquid interfacial surface area increases dramatically compared to simple surface aeration. Additionally, surface agitation and gas entrainment further expand the area for oxygen transfer.</p>
      <p><strong>Step 4: Final Conclusion</strong><br/>The air bubbles dramatically increase the surface area for oxygen transfer into the culture medium, improving aerobic growth and product yield.</p>
    ]]></text></generalfeedback>
